Files in this folder are based on libzip 1.12 (7 October 2026).

Original source: https://github.com/nih-at/libzip, tag v1.12.

Only the library is kept: lib/*.c and lib/*.h in src/, zip.h in include/,
and LICENSE in docs/. The tools, regression tests, man pages and CMake
files are left out, and so are the parts behind options that are off:
bzip2, xz and zstd compression (zip_algorithm_bzip2.c, _xz.c, _zstd.c), the
crypto back ends and WinZip AES encryption (zip_crypto.h,
zip_crypto_{commoncrypto,gnutls,openssl,win}.{c,h}, zip_winzip_aes.c,
zip_source_winzip_aes_*.c) and the UWP random source (zip_random_uwp.c).
zip_crypto.c stays: libzip always builds it, and its _zip_crypto_clear
wipes passwords. Deflate and stored entries remain, as in the old copy;
traditional PKWARE encryption (new since libzip 0.10) is built too, though
revZip does not use it.

Three files are written for OXT-Beyond's build, because gyp runs no
configure step: include/zipconf.h (CMake makes it from zipconf.h.in),
src/config.h (from config.h.in, with the answers for MSVC, macOS, Linux
and Android) and src/zip_err_str.c (CMake makes it from zip.h's error
codes; the messages are libzip's). libzip.gyp builds the Win32 file and
random sources on Windows and the stdio and /dev/urandom (or arc4random)
ones elsewhere. Windows links advapi32: CryptGenRandom seeds the key of
every archive's name table and makes PKWARE encryption headers, and
GetSecurityInfo copies the original file's permissions to the temporary
file that zip_close writes.

Two local changes, marked "OXT-Beyond" in the sources:

- In src/zip_dirent.c, _zip_dirent_read keeps a name (or comment) whose
  entry is flagged UTF-8 but is not valid UTF-8 as the bytes it is, unless
  the archive is opened with ZIP_CHECKCONS, rather than refusing the whole
  archive as inconsistent. Some writers flag names in their own code page;
  the old copy read every name as it was, so such archives opened, and
  still do.
- In src/zip_hash.c, _zip_hash_new seeds the name table's key from
  zip_random_uint32 when zip_secure_random fails, rather than failing.
  Every zip_open makes a name table, so without /dev/urandom (a chroot or
  a sandbox) or a working CryptGenRandom no archive could be opened or
  made at all; the key only guards lookups against names made to collide,
  and zip_random_uint32 is what libzip itself falls back to for temporary
  file names.

The old copy (libzip 0.6.1, July 2005) was modified for LiveCode: a
progress callback inside zip_fread and zip_close, made-by and
external-attribute setters, and Windows wide-character paths.
libzip 1.12 has the last two itself (zip_file_set_external_attributes;
UTF-8 paths become UTF-16 on Windows), and revzip/src/revzip.cpp now
reports the same progress messages from its own read loops and from a
layered source over each item it adds.
